  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 110 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nNortherly winds stay elevated above 25 mph with occasional gusts \nabove 35 mph through Friday afternoon, as CAA behind Thursday's \nfront continues. Highs will struggle to rise above the mid 70s F \nfor most of the area north of the Rio Grande basin, remaining in \nthe 60s F for western higher terrain and northern Lea County, and \nstaying largely within the 80s F even for the southernmost parts \nof the forecast area near the Rio Grande. Following the relatively\ndry weather Thursday, PoPs again increase into the 25% to 35% \nrange northeast of the Marfa Plateau (especially from the \nGuadalupes into Lower Trans Pecos and northeast Permian Basin) in \nthe more humid air behind Thursday's cold front. Persistent \nnortherly/northeasterly winds decreasing with time overnight then \nallow for more efficient overnight cooling, dropping lows Friday \nnight below 60 F north of the Rio Grande basin and into the 40s F \nfor the Guadalupes as well as peaks and foothills of the Marfa \nPlateau. Highs Saturday rise to similar levels as highs Friday \nunder continued northeasterly winds. Rain chances remain in the \nforecast over parts of the area to begin the weekend, due to \nsurface high pressure remaining over the Rockies and core of \nridging suppressing rain chances from sinking motion redeveloping \nfarther west of the forecast area rather than directly overhead.\n\nRedevelopment of mid to upper ridging west of the area late \nweekend through early next week allows for a moderation in \ntemperatures beginning Sunday, following similar lows Saturday \nnight to Friday night. Highs rise back into the 70s and 80s F by \nearly next week, and there are indications of 90s F for southern \nand eastern portions of the lowlands by mid-week next week as near\nsurface winds return to southeasterly. However, as a result of \nsurface and mid-level ridging remaining offset away from the \nforecast area, daily showers/storms will remain in the forecast \neach afternoon/evening. Timing, coverage, and rainfall amounts in \nany storms late weekend through early next week are uncertain at \nthis time.",
